The two tables coexist on original CIRA page 4 for 9M FY2025/26. K-12 normalized net profit is initially -EGP321.5 million and becomes +49.3 million under management's weighted allocation: 49.3 - (-321.5) = 370.8 million uplift. Higher Education moves from 1,505.0 to 1,134.2, an equal 370.8 reduction, leaving total normalized net profit at 1,183.5 in both views. The footnote identifies 25.5 million G&A and 345.3 million finance cost reclassification. Only the overhead component changes EBITDA allocation; it would be wrong to infer the entire financing reclassification is EBITDA improvement.

This is a simultaneous management segmentation alternative, not a later statutory restatement or new group cash earnings. Allocation can help interpret school economics, but the positive adjusted K-12 bottom line is allocation-sensitive. Education's recurring tuition and enrollment growth are supportive; counterevidence on the page includes K-12 normalized EBITDA declining in both presentations and utilization easing despite expansion. Teacher/staff growth and financing burden need scrutiny. Cash collection, development capex, debt terms, normalization reconciliation and allocation drivers remain missing. The retained branded IR-service PDF's issuer-to-service link is unresolved; visual consistency does not clear provenance. I would use these figures only as provisional comparative research and wait for issuer-link reconciliation and cash/debt diligence before an investment decision.
